{"chunk_id": "960f769d2ac1", "text": "This section recounts how the river goddess Ganga becomes the wife of King Santanu of the Kuru line. Earlier, a celestial maiden had promised King Pratipa that she would marry his son; Pratipa later instructs Santanu to accept her without questioning her actions or origins. When Santanu meets the radiant maiden by the Ganges, he asks her to marry him. She agrees on one strict condition: he must never question or interfere with anything she does. Santanu accepts, and they live happily together. However, each time a child is born, Ganga throws the newborn into the river, claiming it is for the king\u2019s good. Bound by his promise, Santanu remains silent through seven such deaths. When she attempts to drown the eighth child, he finally protests, breaking the pact. Ganga then reveals her divine identity and the cosmic purpose behind the events.\nSantanu marries the mysterious Ganga under a vow of non\u2011interference and silently watches her drown seven newborn sons before stopping her at the eighth.\nThemes: divine marriage pact, fate and curses, parental grief, duty versus promise, divine identity revealed", "vector": [0.02067720517516136, -0.017171243205666542, 0.0045351735316216946, 0.02033192105591297, -0.018632059916853905, 0.01354576088488102, -0.028313295915722847, 0.026626715436577797, -0.021819299086928368, -0.02523229829967022, 0.0062914746813476086, 0.007914973422884941, 0.00824033748358488, 0.019920235499739647, 0.00715136481449008, 0.008412979543209076, 0.046453990042209625, -0.009130108170211315, 0.018193814903497696, -0.0184726994484663, -0.005438224412500858, -0.007337287068367004, 0.003419640474021435, -0.0050398195162415504, -0.045763421803712845, 0.021261531859636307, 0.027755528688430786, -0.02488701418042183, 0.003419640474021435, -0.007490008603781462, -0.032562945038080215, -0.012881753034889698, -0.024116765707731247, -0.02484717406332493, -0.009926917962729931, 0.003285178914666176, 0.0015305380802601576